Allocate debug-variable instructions with their trailing objects' padding.

AllocStackInst and DebugValueInst keep their debug variable in
llvm::TrailingObjects (SILType, SILLocation, const SILDebugScope *,
SILDIExprElement, Operand, char), which places each type at its own
alignment. allocateDebugVarCarryingInst summed the sizes without that
padding. On a 64-bit host every trailing type is a multiple of 8 bytes and
nothing is lost; on wasm32 SILDIExprElement is 8-byte aligned (its uint64_t)
after 4-byte objects, so an instruction with a debug expression was up to 4
bytes too small and its last element overwrote the next heap block's header.

Symptom: at -O and -Osize (where salvageDebugInfo and SROA create
debug_values with fragment expressions), dlmalloc traps in free with "memory
access out of bounds", usually from SILModule::flushDeletedInsts, depending
only on the heap's layout: a four-line program crashed or not depending on
its identifiers' names. Found by padding every allocateInst by 16 bytes
(the traps went away), then watching the corrupted header: the write came
from DebugValueInst::create under salvageDebugInfo in SILCombine.

Upstream: the same sum is on main (getDebugVarTrailingDataSize); not reported.

diff --git a/lib/SIL/IR/SILInstructions.cpp b/lib/SIL/IR/SILInstructions.cpp
index ea93eb5..da39308 100644
--- a/lib/SIL/IR/SILInstructions.cpp
+++ b/lib/SIL/IR/SILInstructions.cpp
@@ -155,18 +155,40 @@ collectTypeDependentOperands(SmallVectorImpl<SILValue> &typeDependentOperands,
 // SILInstruction Subclasses
 //===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
 
+/// The size of an instruction carrying a debug variable: the instruction, then
+/// its trailing objects in the order AllocStackInst and DebugValueInst declare
+/// them (SILType, SILLocation, const SILDebugScope *, SILDIExprElement,
+/// Operand, char), each placed at its own alignment as llvm::TrailingObjects
+/// places it. Summing the sizes alone is short by that padding wherever a type
+/// is more aligned than the one before it: on a 32-bit host SILDIExprElement
+/// (8-byte aligned by its uint64_t) follows 4-byte objects, so an instruction
+/// with a debug expression was allocated up to 4 bytes too small and wrote its
+/// last element over the next heap block's header. (DebugValueInst has no
+/// Operand objects; aligning for them only rounds up.)
+template <typename INST>
+static size_t debugVarCarryingInstSize(std::optional<SILDebugVariable> Var,
+                                       size_t numOperands) {
+  size_t size = sizeof(INST);
+  auto append = [&size](size_t align, size_t bytes) {
+    size = llvm::alignTo(size, align) + bytes;
+  };
+  append(alignof(SILType), Var && Var->Type ? sizeof(SILType) : 0);
+  append(alignof(SILLocation), Var && Var->Loc ? sizeof(SILLocation) : 0);
+  append(alignof(const SILDebugScope *),
+         Var && Var->Scope ? sizeof(const SILDebugScope *) : 0);
+  append(alignof(SILDIExprElement),
+         sizeof(SILDIExprElement) * (Var ? Var->DIExpr.getNumElements() : 0));
+  append(alignof(Operand), sizeof(Operand) * numOperands);
+  append(alignof(char), Var ? Var->Name.size() : 0);
+  return size;
+}
+
 template <typename INST>
 static void *allocateDebugVarCarryingInst(SILModule &M,
                                           std::optional<SILDebugVariable> Var,
                                           ArrayRef<SILValue> Operands = {}) {
-  return M.allocateInst(
-      sizeof(INST) + (Var ? Var->Name.size() : 0) +
-          (Var && Var->Type ? sizeof(SILType) : 0) +
-          (Var && Var->Loc ? sizeof(SILLocation) : 0) +
-          (Var && Var->Scope ? sizeof(const SILDebugScope *) : 0) +
-          sizeof(SILDIExprElement) * (Var ? Var->DIExpr.getNumElements() : 0) +
-          sizeof(Operand) * Operands.size(),
-      alignof(INST));
+  return M.allocateInst(debugVarCarryingInstSize<INST>(Var, Operands.size()),
+                        alignof(INST));
 }
 
